menu "PixelClip IMU (QMI8658C)"

    config PIXELCLIP_IMU_ENABLE
        bool "Use the QMI8658C accelerometer"
        default y
        help
            I2C on SCL=GPIO39 / SDA=GPIO40, 400 kHz. When disabled (or the chip is not found) live
            scenes use a fixed downward gravity.

    config PIXELCLIP_IMU_FLIP_X
        bool "Flip display-frame X (left/right) of the gravity vector"
        default n
        help
            Bench fix for the axis assumption in components/imu/imu.c (IMU_AXIS_MAP). With the clip
            upright and its RIGHT edge tilted down, GET /imu must report g[0] > 0. If it is negative,
            enable this.

    config PIXELCLIP_IMU_FLIP_Y
        bool "Flip display-frame Y (up/down) of the gravity vector"
        default n
        help
            Upright clip (display landscape, readable): GET /imu must report g[1] close to +1.
            If it reads -1, enable this.

    config PIXELCLIP_IMU_FLIP_Z
        bool "Flip display-frame Z (out of / into the glass) of the gravity vector"
        default n
        help
            Clip lying face up (glass toward the sky): GET /imu must report g[2] close to -1.
            If it reads +1, enable this.

    config PIXELCLIP_IMU_SWAP_XY
        bool "Swap display-frame X and Y (chip rotated 90 degrees on the board)"
        default n
        help
            Applied before the flips. Enable if tilting the clip left/right moves g[1] instead of
            g[0] (upright then reads g[0] = +-1 instead of g[1]).

    config PIXELCLIP_IMU_LOW_POWER
        bool "Accelerometer low-power mode (128 Hz ODR) instead of normal mode (62.5 Hz)"
        default n
        help
            The QMI8658C low-power accelerometer rates are 128/21/11/3 Hz; only 128 Hz is fast enough
            for the 50 Hz sampling. Normal mode at 62.5 Hz is the default (verified register values
            from the Waveshare demo family); try this on the bench to save a little current.

endmenu
